# Every text file is checked out with Unix line endings (LF) on every computer, Windows included,
# whatever git's own core.autocrlf says there (Git for Windows turns it on by default). The repository
# already stores LF everywhere; this keeps the copy on disk the same, so a test that reads a file
# sees the same text on Windows as on CI. Files git sees as binary are left alone.
# A clone made before this line still has Windows line endings on disk. Commit your work first: the
# command throws away uncommitted changes. Then run `git rm -r --cached . && git reset --hard` once
# (CONTRIBUTING.md, "Line endings on Windows", says more).
* text=auto eol=lf

# Almost every pull request adds a line under [Unreleased] in these two logs, so two branches
# meeting there used to conflict on lines that both belong. git's built-in union merge keeps both
# sides' lines instead (ours first, then theirs) and never stops for a conflict in these files.
# It can't tell a reworded line from a new one: where both sides changed the same lines, both
# versions are kept, so read the merged section once.
# Not docs/task-list.md: its lines are ticked in place, and a union merge would keep both the
# ticked and the unticked copy of a line.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
docs/privacy-log.md merge=union
